window.docContents[64].resolve({"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-counting-a-finset-finset-card":{"contents":"The most basic notion is the cardinality of a Finset. A Finset α is by\nconstruction a finite collection of elements of α, so it always has a well-defined\nsize: a natural number. This is Finset.card, written either s.card or (after\nopen Finset) with the prefix notation #s.\n\nsection finset\n\n\nopen Finset\n\n\n\n#check Finset.card -- {α : Type u_1} → Finset α → ℕ\n#eval ({0, 1, 2} : Finset ℕ).card -- 3\n#eval #K[4].edgeFinset            -- 6\n#eval #K[4, 3].edgeFinset         -- 12\n\n\nend finset\n\n\nIn particular, the degree of a vertex (see Finite Neighbourhoods\nbelow) is literally #(G.neighborFinset v), and #G.edgeFinset is the number of\nedges of G.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Decidability, Finiteness and Degrees\u0009Finite Types\u0009Cardinality","header":"4.2.4.1. Counting a  Finset :  Finset.card","id":"/Decidability___-Finiteness-and-Degrees/#section-counting-a-finset-finset-card"}});